w: 1.~Why dost thou boast, O _ might- y man, of mis- chief _ and of ill? _ The good- ness of Al- might- y God en- du- _ reth _ ev- er still.
w: 2.~Thy tongue mis- chie- vous _ ca- lum- nies de- vi- seth _ sub- ti- lely, _ Like to a ra- zor sharp to cut, work- ing _ de- _ ceit- ful- ly.
w: * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * *
w: 4.~So God shall thee de- _ stroy for aye, re- move thee, _ pluck thee out _ Quite from thy house, out of the land of life _ he _ shall thee root.
w: 5.~The right- eous shall it _ see, and fear, and laugh at _ him they shall: _ Lo, this the man is that did not make God _ his _ strength at all:
w: 6.~But he in his a- _ bun- dant wealth his con- fi- _ dence did place; _ And he took strength un- to him- self from his _ own _ wick- ed- ness.
w: 7.~But I am in the _ house of God like to an _ ol- ive green: _ My con- fi- dence for ev- er hath up- on _ God’s _ mer- cy been.
w: 8.~And I for ev- er _ will thee praise, be- cause thou _ hast done this: _ I on thy name will wait; for good be- fore _ thy _ saints it is.
